<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Choiseul Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lauru</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Choiseul is an island in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>, lying south-east of Bougainville. Part of the German Solomon Islands from 1886, it was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with Santa Isabel and other islands under an agreement of 1899, taking effect in 1900. It is now Choiseul Province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choiseul-island.jpg" alt="Choiseul Island" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sadalmelik · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Choiseul_Topography.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Choiseul Island, whose native name is Lauru, is the largest island, at 2,971 square kilometres, of Choiseul Province in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>. The provincial headquarters is the town of Taro, on Taro Island. The islanders speak Austronesian languages, among them Vaghua, Varisi, Ririo and Central Eastern Choiseul. In the centre lies the Mount Maetambe to Kolombangara River Important Bird Area, 78,398 hectares of intact forest reaching 800 metres. BirdLife International identified it for its threatened or endemic birds, and it holds the best example of karst limestone forest in the Solomons, with caves and subterranean rivers. Mount Maetambae is an extinct volcano.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1768 the French explorer Louis Antoine de Bougainville named the island after Étienne François, duc de Choiseul, then French foreign minister. The first recorded European sighting was by the Spanish expedition of Álvaro de Mendaña in April 1568, from the brigantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, commanded by Pedro Ortega Valencia; they charted it as San Marcos. In the nineteenth century the islanders suffered attacks from blackbirding, the often brutal recruitment or kidnapping of labourers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations of Queensland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the North Solomon Islands, including Choiseul. Under the Treaty of Berlin, signed on 14 November 1899, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> transferred them, except Bougainville and its surrounding islands, to the British Solomon Islands Protectorate in 1900, in exchange for the British giving up all claims to Samoa. Missionaries converted most of the population to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and British and Australian firms began large-scale coconut planting. The Austrian anthropologist Hugo Bernatzik visited in 1932 and documented some of the few remaining ancestral customs. Japanese forces occupied the island in 1942, and US Marines raided it in October and November 1943. Since independence in July 1978 it has been part of Choiseul Province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Choiseul Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was extended in 1898 and 1899, and in 1900 Germany transferred Santa Isabel, <b>Choiseul</b> and other northern islands to Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-solomon-islands-protectorate">British Solomon Islands Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
